---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-time-of-day`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Time of Day',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and Time of Day', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Time of Day: Why Noon Sun Makes Vitamin D and Evening Sun Doesn't"
  description="Why UVB availability is compressed around solar noon, when to actually go outside for vitamin D, and why early-morning and late-afternoon sun is nearly pure UVA."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-time-of-day", label: "Vitamin D & Time of Day" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Time of Day</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      The sun's height above the horizon (solar zenith angle) controls how much UVB
      reaches the ground. At sunrise and sunset the sun is low, UVB traverses more
      atmosphere, and gets absorbed almost entirely. At solar noon, UVB peaks. The
      window where meaningful vitamin D synthesis occurs is much narrower than the
      total daylight window.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">The 35° rule</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      The sun needs to be at least ~35° above the horizon for usable UVB to reach the
      ground. Below that angle, the atmospheric path length filters out virtually all
      UVB while transmitting UVA (which tans but doesn't make vitamin D). This is why
      sunrise and sunset feel gentle — they are. And why morning coffee in the sun
      makes no vitamin D.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Solar noon hits differently by latitude and season</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Equator (0°) at any equinox</strong> — sun straight overhead at noon (90°).</li>
      <li><strong>40° N in summer solstice</strong> — 73° at noon; strong UVB from about 10 AM to 3 PM.</li>
      <li><strong>40° N in winter solstice</strong> — 27° at noon; sub-threshold; no meaningful UVB all day.</li>
      <li><strong>60° N (Oslo) in summer</strong> — 53° at noon; usable UVB in a narrower window.</li>
      <li><strong>60° N in winter</strong> — 7° at noon; effectively no UVB.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The vitamin D window</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      At temperate latitudes in summer, the vitamin-D-synthesising window is roughly
      10 AM to 3 PM (some conservative sources say 11 AM–1 PM). At the winter solstice
      above 40°, the window is closed entirely. In spring and autumn, it opens for
      1–3 hours around noon in temperate zones. Adjust your outdoor time accordingly if
      you're relying on sun for any of your vitamin D.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Morning walks and evening jogs</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Great for cardiovascular fitness, mood, and circadian entrainment (early morning
      light regulates the biological clock). Not effective for vitamin D synthesis.
      If you exercise outdoors early or late, you still need to supplement or get
      midday sun for vitamin D.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Daylight saving time confusion</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Solar noon (when the sun is highest) is when it's highest — not necessarily 12:00
      PM on the clock. During Daylight Saving Time, solar noon in most US locations
      falls around 1:00 PM. When people say "midday," they usually mean solar noon
      ± 1–2 hours. Check a solar-position app if you want to be precise.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Shadow rule of thumb</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      If your shadow is shorter than your height, you're in usable UVB territory. If
      your shadow is longer than your height, the sun angle is too low for meaningful
      vitamin D synthesis. A crude but functional field guide.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical timing</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Aim for outdoor time between 11 AM and 2 PM in summer for vitamin D.</li>
      <li>Fair skin: 5–15 minutes of face and arms is often enough. Stop before pinkening.</li>
      <li>Darker skin: 20–45 minutes may be needed for equivalent synthesis.</li>
      <li>Winter above 40°: don't rely on any time-of-day; supplement.</li>
      <li>See our <a href="/best-time-of-day-for-vitamin-d" class="text-amber-700 underline">best time of day page</a> for the sun-vs-supplement timing question.</li>
    </ol>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Use our
      <a href="/" class="text-amber-700 underline">calculator</a> for your location and
      season to see when UVB is actually available.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-time-of-day" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
